@@@@ mark-error-c-sliding-window-last-window
situation: mark-error
topic: Sliding windows
options: {"strategy":"code"}
voice: typos
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["n - 1","last (element|window)"],"must":["\\b13\\b","i < n`"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"askMustNot":["\\b21\\b"]}
@@ tutor
A sliding window avoids adding up the whole window again each time it moves: when it moves one place right, add the element that enters and subtract the one that leaves.

<note>Fixed-size sliding window: keep the sum of the current k elements; moving right by one is `sum += a[i] - a[i - k]`. All the window sums together cost $O(n)$ instead of $O(nk)$.</note>

<solution>Build the first window's sum, then slide: for i from k to n − 1, sum += a[i] − a[i − k], keeping the largest. On {2, 9, -3, 4, 1, 8} with k = 3 the window sums are 8, 10, 2, 13, so it returns 13.</solution>
<check lang="python">
a, k = [2, 9, -3, 4, 1, 8], 3
sums = [sum(a[i:i + k]) for i in range(len(a) - k + 1)]
assert sums == [8, 10, 2, 13] and max(sums) == 13
</check>

<ask type="code" lang="c">Write `int max_window(const int *a, int n, int k)` that returns the largest sum of k consecutive elements (assume 1 ≤ k ≤ n), and print its result for {2, 9, -3, 4, 1, 8} with k = 3.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int max_window(const int *a, int n, int k) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += a[i];
    int best = sum;
    for (int i = k; i < n - 1; i++) {
        sum += a[i] - a[i - k];
        if (sum > best) best = sum;
    }
    return best;
}

int main(void) {
    int a[] = {2, 9, -3, 4, 1, 8};
    printf("%d\n", max_window(a, 6, 3));
    return 0;
}
```
it prints 10, thats the window with 9, -3 and 4. used the slide trick so its O(n) not O(nk)
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">`sum += a[i] - a[i - k];` is the right update and the first window is built correctly, so the method is $O(n)$ as you say. The first wrong line is `for (int i = k; i < n - 1; i++)`: it stops one short, so the window that ends at the last element is never formed.</grade>

I ran it: it does print 10. The four windows of your test sum to 8, 10, 2 and 13. Your loop runs for i = 3 and i = 4 only, so it never reaches 4 + 1 + 8 = 13, which is the true maximum. With the bound `i < n` the same program prints 13; nothing else needs to change.

<card>In a sliding-window loop over a[0..n-1] that adds a[i] and drops a[i - k], what is the loop bound? :: i < n. The entering index i must reach n - 1, or the last window is skipped.</card>

<solution>With `for (int i = k; i < n; i++)` the program prints 13 for k = 3. For k = 6 the loop body never runs and the first sum is returned: 2 + 9 − 3 + 4 + 1 + 8 = 21.</solution>
<check lang="python">
a = [2, 9, -3, 4, 1, 8]
best = lambda k: max(sum(a[i:i + k]) for i in range(len(a) - k + 1))
assert best(3) == 13 and best(6) == 21
</check>

<ask type="code" lang="c">Correct the loop bound, then print `max_window` for the same array twice: with k = 3 and with k = 6.</ask>
@@ verify
src = block(learner, 'c')
out, err, cc, code = run_c_full(src, 'c')
assert out.strip() == '10' and code == 0 and not cc.strip(), (out, err, cc)   # their program, their claim
a = [2, 9, -3, 4, 1, 8]
sums = [sum(a[i:i + 3]) for i in range(4)]
assert sums == [8, 10, 2, 13] and 9 - 3 + 4 == 10 and 4 + 1 + 8 == 13
# their loop visits i = 3 and 4 only
assert list(range(3, 6 - 1)) == [3, 4]
# the corrected program
fixed = src.replace('i < n - 1', 'i < n').replace('printf("%d\\n", max_window(a, 6, 3));', 'printf("%d\\n", max_window(a, 6, 3));\n    printf("%d\\n", max_window(a, 6, 6));')
assert fixed != src and 'max_window(a, 6, 6)' in fixed
assert run_c(fixed).split() == ['13', '21']
