@@ tutor
A list of records is in name order: (Ada, York), (Bea, York), (Cy, Leeds). You now sort it by city with selection sort, which finds the smallest remaining key and swaps it into the next position, one position at a time.

<ask type="predict">After the sort by city, will the two York records still be in name order? Say why.</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
I think yes? They were already in name order, and the sort only compares cities, it never even looks at the names. So it has no reason to reorder Ada and Bea, they tie on city so it should just leave them. Unless I'm missing something about how the swap works
@@ reply
<grade verdict="wrong" score="0.2">"it has no reason to reorder Ada and Bea" assumes a record only moves when a comparison says it should. Selection sort also moves records as a side effect of its swaps, and your last sentence points at exactly that.</grade>

Run the first pass. The smallest city is Leeds, held by Cy in position 2, so position 0 and position 2 are swapped:

(Cy, Leeds), (Bea, York), (Ada, York)

That is already in city order, so the sort ends there, with Bea ahead of Ada. No comparison ever involved a name, as you said; Ada was carried over Bea by a swap whose purpose was to place Cy.

<card>Can sorting by city disturb the name order of records in the same city? :: Yes, if the sort moves a record past others with an equal key. Selection sort's long swap does: (Ada, York), (Bea, York), (Cy, Leeds) becomes (Cy, Leeds), (Bea, York), (Ada, York).</card>

<note>A sort can reorder records with equal keys without ever comparing them unfavourably: any move that carries a record past an equal-keyed one does it. Selection sort's swap is such a move.</note>

<ask type="predict">Insertion sort handles the same three records differently: it takes Cy out, shifts Bea one place right, then Ada one place right, and puts Cy at the front. Predict whether Ada and Bea keep their order this time, and say what differs from the swap.</ask>
@@ verify
recs = [('Ada', 'York'), ('Bea', 'York'), ('Cy', 'Leeds')]
def selection(a):
    a = a[:]
    for i in range(len(a)):
        m = min(range(i, len(a)), key=lambda j: (a[j][1], j))    # first smallest key
        a[i], a[m] = a[m], a[i]
    return a
def insertion(a):
    a = a[:]
    for i in range(1, len(a)):
        x, j = a[i], i - 1
        while j >= 0 and a[j][1] > x[1]:
            a[j + 1] = a[j]; j -= 1
        a[j + 1] = x
    return a
fmt = lambda a: ', '.join(f'({n}, {c})' for n, c in a)
assert fmt(recs) in tutor[0]
s = selection(recs)
assert fmt(s) == '(Cy, Leeds), (Bea, York), (Ada, York)' and fmt(s) in reply
assert [c for _, c in s] == sorted(c for _, c in recs)             # in city order after one swap
# after the first swap (positions 0 and 2) nothing else moves
a = recs[:]; a[0], a[2] = a[2], a[0]
assert a == s
# the predict question's answer: insertion sort keeps Ada before Bea
assert fmt(insertion(recs)) == '(Cy, Leeds), (Ada, York), (Bea, York)'
assert fmt(insertion(recs)) not in reply
